Design of match test:
- normalize(text): lowercase, collapse whitespace, strip punctuation.
- For a response body, decode bytes → text; check if any of: doi (lowercased), arxiv_id, or normalized title's first 6 significant words present. For XML API query responses, the entry titles are in <title>; checking for title token substring in the whole body is fine but may match query echo (the query itself contains title words!). Careful: for arxiv-api-query responses, the arXiv API echoes the query in <opensearch:Query> or not? It returns the query in the request echo? The response may contain the query terms. A naive substring test would produce a false "identifying". Better match rule: parse the response for record-level fields:
  - For arXiv API XML: extract all `<entry><title>` texts, and each entry's `<id>` (abs URL) / doi. Match if any entry title normalized contains the row title's first 6 words or the entry id contains the arxiv_id.
  - For INSPIRE JSON: parse JSON, look at hits.hits[*].metadata.titles[].title and .dois/.arxiv_eprints.
  - For HTML pages (arxiv abs, publisher): check `<title>` tag / body for DOI or arXiv id and title tokens; note that the arXiv abs page — the locator IS the id, so it identifies by construction if HTTP 200 and the page title contains the row title.
